     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg of ibuprofen a day.\n- The usual highest single dose for an adult is 800 mg. This order asks for twice that in each dose.\n- The usual highest total for an adult is 3,200 mg a day. This order is double that.\n- Doses this high carry a real risk of stomach bleeding, sudden kidney damage and heart problems, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n**2. A clinician needs to place the order.** Medication orders have to come from a licensed prescriber who is responsible for the patient's care, such as the attending physician, resident, NP or PA on the medicine service. You've told me you aren't a clinician, so I can't enter a medication order on your behalf, even at a normal dose.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- Ask the patient's medical team to review the order. If they want ibuprofen, a typical adult regimen is 400\u2013800 mg by mouth every 6\u20138 hours, up to 3,200 mg a day. That's for the prescriber to decide.\n- If \"1600\" was a typo or a mix-up with the daily total (for example, 400 mg four times daily = 1,600 mg a day), the prescriber can confirm that and enter it.\n- A pharmacist on the medicine service can also help sort out what was intended.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms an appropriate order, I can help them enter it."
      }
     ],
